# /delete-comment

Comments are banned in this repo{{RULE_SENTENCE}}. `{{LINT_DIR}}/baseline.json` lists a
fingerprint for every comment line that is still allowed. The check fails when a file has a
comment line the baseline does not list (a new one, or an old one reworded) and when the
baseline lists lines that are gone. This skill is how the list gets shorter.

The script does everything that needs no judgment. Your one job is the classification in
step 3 and what follows from it: where each decision goes, and which issues to file.

## 1. Pick the scope

```bash
{{RUN}} count            # what is left, by language
{{RUN}} count <path>     # one directory or file
```

Take the path the user named. With no path, take the directory with the most comment lines.
`strip` edits TypeScript, JavaScript, Python and shell. YAML, SQL, Dockerfiles and
`.conf`/`.env`/`.properties` files are counted by the lint and edited by hand.

## 2. List the comments

```bash
{{RUN}} scan <path> > "$SCRATCH/blocks.json"
{{RUN}} scan <path> --directives > "$SCRATCH/directives.json"
```

Each block has `id` (`<file>:<first line>`), `start`, `end`, `own` (true when the comment has
its lines to itself) and `text`. Lint directives, shebangs, type pragmas and inline script
metadata (the `# /// script` block `uv run` reads) are in the second list. They are never
stripped and never counted.

## 3. Classify every block

Follow [classify-brief.md](classify-brief.md). It defines the four categories and the three
output files. Up to about 300 blocks, do it yourself. Above that, split the block list by
directory into chunks of about 250 blocks and give each chunk to one subagent with the brief.
The subagents write their output files and nothing else.

Check that every block id got exactly one category before going on.

## 4. Move what has to survive

A decision has three possible homes. Take the first one that fits.

1. **The code itself.** If a test, a type, an assertion or a better name can state the rule,
   write that. A rule the build enforces cannot go stale. Keep it small: one test or one type
   per rule, and nothing that changes behaviour. Tell the user which rules you encoded.
2. **An existing decision record** that the comment corrects or extends. Edit that record.
3. **A new entry.** {{DECISION_HOWTO}}

Excuses: {{ISSUES_HOWTO}} Group them by area. One issue per area with a checklist of the
shortcuts is enough. Describe what the code does today and the change that removes it.

Tautology and stale comments have nothing to move.

## 5. Look at the directives

Read `directives.json`. A suppression (`eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`,
`# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `# nosec`, `# shellcheck disable`) that hides a correctness or
safety rule is an excuse in a different spelling. Do not delete it here. List each one in the
matching issue from step 4 with the rule it silences.

## 6. Find code that reads comments

Stripping passes its syntax check and still breaks anything that reads source as text. Before
stripping, look for both kinds in the scope:

```bash
grep -rnE 'sed -n .*"\$0"|grep .*"\$0"' <path>
grep -rnE "indexOf\(['\"\`](//|/\*|#)|['\"\`]// " <path>
```

A script that prints its header as `--help` needs its usage text moved into a heredoc. A test
that slices a source file between two comment markers needs code anchors, or it keeps passing
while checking nothing. A module that hashes its own source will report a new hash.

## 7. Strip, verify, lower the baseline

```bash
{{RUN}} strip <path> --dry-run
{{RUN}} strip <path>
{{RUN}} baseline
{{RUN}} check
```

`strip` rewrites a file only when the result parses to the same syntax tree (TypeScript
tokens, Python AST, `bash --pretty-print`). A file it reports as `SKIP` is untouched. Fix
that one by hand. To strip only some blocks, pass `--ids <file.json>` with an array of ids.

Then run the suites that cover the scope{{VERIFY_SENTENCE}}. `baseline` only shortens the
list, and it refuses when a file has a comment line it does not know.

## 8. Report

Say how many blocks fell in each category, how many lines were removed, which rules became
tests or types, which records and issues were written, what was skipped and why, and the new
total from `count`.
